<div class="row row-cols-auto align-items-center" id="how-section">
	<RadzenIcon Icon="line_end_arrow" />
	<p class="pt-3">HOW</p>
</div>
<div class="min-vh-100">
	<div class="w-75 pb-4">
		<h1>How does it work?</h1>
	</div>
	<div class="container-100 pb-3">
		<div class="row">
			<div class="col fs-1">
				Imagine a 2D square grid, just like one from middle school, with an 
				x-axis and a y-axis, and a player is a point on that grid. Imagine 
				the grid has numbers in different squares. Raycasting works by 
				getting the player's line of sight and shooting rays from the player 
				until they hit an object; depending on the value it hits, it can tell 
				the program whether that value is a wall, door, etc. The length of the 
				ray can determine how far away that object is. 
			</div>			
		</div>
		<div class="h6 mt-lg-5">
			This is a very simplified explanation; in practice, it is much more 
			complex and involves extensive calculus. If you wish to research more, here 
			is a great resource: <a href="https://lodev.org/cgtutor/raycasting.html" target="_blank">Learn More</a>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>
<hr />

